## Why the old six-helper DTO is not an executable recipe

Ephemeral cat/readlink/hash tools can finish before their own full stat/argv/env/exe proof is captured. Adding an observer changes the actual inventory; it cannot be hidden behind `[1,candidate,helper]`. A BASHPID prefix or fixed desired tuple is insufficient. The all-slash argv constraint also cannot represent literal Bash flags/programs. Do not convert synthetic six-distinct helper tuples into claimed observations or grant generic helper/prefix authority.

Use a new source family with two persistent builtin-only peer readers. This is a finite mutual proof, not recursive arbitrary helpers. It avoids own-proc access and extra tool children altogether. Every actor remains alive on its connected stdin throughout both proofs. Its runtime feasibility remains unproven until the separately admitted process-only gate.

